/-!
# The refutation's own subgraph of `P₅ ⊔ (0, δ)`, as an edge list on `Fin 125`

**What this is, and why it is not the whole graph.** Level five of the 2-adic tower has 1,024 vertices and 24,576
edges, and adding the depth-4 pair `(0, δ)` makes it 4-uncolourable. CaDiCaL's proof of that uses **125 of those
vertices and 778 of those edges** — measured by backward reachability from the empty clause through the committed
LRAT (`data/fs38/lrat/`), which touches 861 of the formula's 99,335 clauses. A subgraph that refuses refutes every
graph it maps into, so this list is all the kernel needs, and it is **31× cheaper**: 3,240 clauses against 99,335,
which is the difference between a clause table the kernel checks in seconds and one that cost 853 s at a 33 GB peak.

**Generated, and untrusted in both directions.** `twoAdicFiveCoreIdx` records which vertex of the full level each
index here is, in the generator's own order (`i = 128a + 16b + 4c + d`); `Instances/TwoAdicFive.lean` re-derives in
the kernel that **every** listed pair really is an edge of `P₅`, or else is exactly the added pair `(0, δ)`. The order
is the order the DIMACS was written in and the certificate indexes clauses by position, so it must not change; the
**last** pair is the added one.

Regenerate: take the committed edge list and LRAT, keep the formula clauses the proof reaches, induce on their
vertices, and re-solve with the manifest's flags (`build/fs38-k3/core_resolve.py` in the phase's scratch; the
resulting CNF and LRAT are provenance, and the artifact a reviewer re-checks is this module plus `Cnf/`).
-/

